{"execute":{"doc":1,"cmd":{"Write":{"PasteMarkdown":{"text":"Lanternfield Robotics\n\nFor immediate release\n\nLanternfield Robotics opens its first school robotics lab in Cardiff\n\nFree after-school programme will reach 600 pupils across eight schools in its first year\n\nCARDIFF, 24 February 2026 — Lanternfield Robotics today opened a robotics lab at Bay View High School, the first of eight it will run with schools across Cardiff this year. The lab offers free after-school sessions where pupils aged 11 to 16 design, build and program their own robots, guided by Lanternfield engineers.\n\nThe programme was shaped with teachers, who asked for hands-on projects that link maths and science lessons to real engineering. Each school receives a full kit of parts, laptops and a trained lab leader for the year.\n\n“Every pupil should get the chance to build something that moves and thinks,” said Gwen Morgan, chief executive of Lanternfield Robotics. “We want these labs to be the place where the next generation of Welsh engineers discovers what they love.”\n\nBay View head teacher Owen Price added: “Within a week, pupils who had never written a line of code were racing their robots down the corridor. The energy has been remarkable.”\n\nThe remaining seven labs will open between April and September 2026. Schools in Cardiff can register their interest at lanternfield.example/schools.\n\nAbout Lanternfield Robotics\n\nLanternfield Robotics designs autonomous machines for warehouses and farms. Founded in 2019, the company employs 140 people at its headquarters in Cardiff Bay.\n\nMedia contact\n\nRhys Llewellyn, Communications Manager · press@lanternfield.example · +44 29 2018 0456\n\n###\n"}}}}}
